We investigate global existence of solutions to a specific class of reaction-diffusion equations, defined by the partial differential equation \begin{equation}\label{eq1}
\begin{split}
\frac{\partial u}{\partial t}(\xi, t)&=\mathfrak{L} u(\xi, t),
\ \ u(\xi, t)=u(\xi \cdot \eta, t), \ \ u(x,y,t)=0 \ \ \text{on} \ \ \partial \Omega,
\end{split}
\end{equation} where $\Omega$ is a bounded domain in $\mathbb{R}^2$, $\eta$ is a vector representing a rough curve, and $\mathfrak{L}$ is a general differential operator. The existence of solutions is established under the assumption that the operator $\mathfrak{L}$ is sectorial, and the rough curve $\eta$ is given by the equation $x = t$, with the initial condition given as a continuous function $u_0(x,y)$.

